## Budget / stop (machine-readable — the runtime enforces this)

These limits are **hard stops**, not suggestions. When any limit is reached, the
loop halts immediately, writes a `budget-exceeded` status to `{{STATE_PATH}}`,
and waits for the student to raise the budget or mark the loop done.

**Delete the keys you are not setting and keep at least one**, with a concrete
number. An omitted key means "no limit on that dimension"; omitting all three
means no budget, which is not allowed. (The template lists all three as a menu —
trim it down.)

```loop-budget
max_iterations: {{MAX_ITERATIONS}}
max_tokens: {{MAX_TOKENS}}
max_minutes: {{MAX_MINUTES}}
```

- `max_iterations` — stop after this many iterations. **This is the dial to set
  first** — it's the only cap that can't overshoot (it advances only when an
  iteration completes).
- `max_tokens` — stop once cumulative tokens spent by the loop reach this. In a
  real agent vault each iteration costs **~150k–200k tokens** (the whole context
  reloads through cache every turn), so set this as a **generous runaway
  backstop** — millions, not hundreds of thousands — or it trips after one or two
  iterations before the goal is reached. Checked at iteration boundaries, so it
  can overshoot by up to one iteration's worth.
- `max_minutes` — stop once wall-clock time across the run reaches this. Also
  checked at iteration boundaries (can overshoot by one iteration).

### Why a hard stop is required

An agent loop cold-starts on every run with no inherent awareness of how many
times it has already fired. Without an external counter in `{{STATE_PATH}}` and a
ceiling checked against it, nothing prevents the loop from running indefinitely —
draining the student's token usage. The `loop-budget` block is the brake the
runtime checks before every iteration; because it lives on disk it survives the
cold start that an in-memory counter would not.
